abstract: >-
  An exact, symmetry-stratified computational search for bilinear
  matrix-multiplication schemes over the field with two elements, run on a single
  consumer laptop. Every scheme is accepted only after a direct check of all n^6
  Brent equations, and every negative result is a solver refutation rather than
  an exhausted budget.

  The rank-47 scheme for 4x4 published by AlphaTensor is exactly invariant under a
  group of order 12, an S3 of permutation sandwiches together with a
  transpose-swap involution, with orbit sizes 12,6,6,6,6,3,3,3,1,1. Searching
  from scratch under that symmetry produced a second, host-verified rank-47
  scheme over F2 that shares no term with AlphaTensor's, has a different
  GL(4,2)^3-invariant factor-rank signature, and is therefore inequivalent to it
  under every change of basis and slot symmetry.

  Both rank-47 schemes are locally rigid and flip-isolated, established by 28,835
  decided SAT instances. A fixed-point restriction lemma for involution-symmetric
  schemes refutes symmetry classes at negligible cost and re-derives the rank of
  <2,2,2> over F2 as 7 by SAT. At 5x5 and 6x6 the published rank-93 and rank-153
  schemes sit in families of 307 and 79 distinct host-verified same-rank schemes,
  where one of each was previously known.

  No published record is improved, and whether a rank-46 scheme for 4x4 over F2
  exists remains open. Every refutation is exact but conditional on a symmetry
  assumption and local to a stated rewrite radius; the paper states the scope of
  each class of result explicitly, including which censuses are partial and why
  undecided instances are never counted as negatives.
keywords:
  - matrix multiplication
  - bilinear complexity
  - tensor rank
  - Brent equations
  - finite fields
  - SAT solving
  - symmetry breaking
  - flip graphs
  - Strassen
  - AlphaTensor
  - certified computation
  - reproducible research
  - negative result
